{"candidates":[{"content":"SRC_004 inherits the algebraic reduction of SRC_002 but contradicts it on causal sufficiency: while SRC_002 depends on arithmetic substitution to derive P=1.25, SRC_004 argues that without a price-determination mechanism (e.g., supply/demand) the derivation is mathematically valid but causally incomplete. This is refuted if algebraic constraint satisfaction is accepted as a complete explanation of price, or if an implicit cost-plus pricing mechanism is inferred from the transaction context.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0d3303c073506b48e3330c9da315a94f77fd9cf9bcf4bf5a37fbfdb5f28461a3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"9809b2fb1504ffb2958a2271af93376021d50257394e93113c7496f18a0a8543"},{"role":"dependence","target":"ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"}],"typicality":0.85},{"content":"SRC_006 reduces to SRC_003 by a directional parsing error of the comparative 'more than': both share the mechanism of subtracting a $10 quantity from the $12.50 total, but where SRC_003 computes the residual 12.50-10=2.50 as the pen price directly, SRC_006 mis-attaches the $10 to the pen's base rather than the notebook's premium. SRC_006 thus depends on the same residual arithmetic as SRC_003 but contradicts the correct directionality. This is refuted if SRC_003's derivation does not use residual subtraction, or if SRC_006's mechanism is shown to be a mis-segmentation rather than a directional inversion.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"07a139da8aba6e75c1856f9a57cd772bffa92bc4248d295dcda03d20d42143f9"},{"role":"dependence","target":"770eaa394b0ecefa294cf24590ec10b969c7a7cac1eac6b9b1dbb2a7d13e2a7a"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"SRC_007 contradicts SRC_002 by inverting the directional assignment of the $10 premium: SRC_002 depends on N=P+10 (notebook above pen) yielding P=1.25, while SRC_007 assumes P=N+10 (pen above notebook) yielding P=11.25. Both share the mechanism of algebraic substitution into N+P=12.50 but differ in which variable receives the additive premium. This is refuted if the English sentence 'the notebook costs $10 more than the pen' is unambiguously parsed as N=P+10, or if the real-world implausibility of an $11.25 pen and $1.25 notebook is treated as a valid disqualifier.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"de58d9a3b82cbe049d5d6f9425614189ad3224c00776777f9958011a355c3de0"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0d3303c073506b48e3330c9da315a94f77fd9cf9bcf4bf5a37fbfdb5f28461a3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"8b9b7aeead16804f8189d267c9a99c15c0f05b8a1cfe12ad3a06990fab69726d"}],"typicality":0.8},{"content":"SRC_006 and SRC_007 share a mechanism of comparative-directionality failure but differ in error type: SRC_006 mis-segments the sentence (dropping 'more than the pen' and treating $10 as the notebook's absolute price), while SRC_007 inverts the comparative (assigning the premium to the pen instead of the notebook). SRC_006 depends on a truncation error, SRC_007 on a directional inversion. This is refuted if both error types collapse to the same misparse, or if either artifact's mechanism is shown to produce a different algebraic structure.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"07a139da8aba6e75c1856f9a57cd772bffa92bc4248d295dcda03d20d42143f9"},{"role":"dependence","target":"de58d9a3b82cbe049d5d6f9425614189ad3224c00776777f9958011a355c3de0"},{"role":"dependence","target":"770eaa394b0ecefa294cf24590ec10b969c7a7cac1eac6b9b1dbb2a7d13e2a7a"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"SRC_002 and SRC_005 are compatible with SRC_004's demand for a causal mechanism if the algebraic substitution itself is abstracted as a causal mechanism: the constraint N+P=12.50 represents the transaction total (a market-clearing condition), and N=P+10 represents a structural price-determination rule (e.g., cost-plus premium). Under this abstraction, SRC_002's derivation integrates with a mechanistic frame by treating the equations as encoding a supply-side pricing rule. This is refuted if algebraic constraints are definitionally incapable of constituting causal mechanisms, or if the school stance requires an empirically grounded pricing model rather than a structural one.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0d3303c073506b48e3330c9da315a94f77fd9cf9bcf4bf5a37fbfdb5f28461a3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"8b9b7aeead16804f8189d267c9a99c15c0f05b8a1cfe12ad3a06990fab69726d"},{"role":"dependence","target":"9809b2fb1504ffb2958a2271af93376021d50257394e93113c7496f18a0a8543"},{"role":"dependence","target":"ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"}],"typicality":0.25},{"content":"SRC_006 contradicts SRC_008 by implying a non-zero pen price ($2.50) while SRC_008 claims P=$0; both are refuted by SRC_002's correct algebraic derivation of P=$1.25. SRC_006's error (mis-segmentation yielding N=$10, P=$2.50) and SRC_008's claim (P=$0) share the failure mode of violating the total-sum constraint when checked against N+P=12.50: SRC_006 is only consistent if N=$10, but then N+P=$12.50 gives P=$2.50, which contradicts the stated premium N=P+10. This is refuted if SRC_008 is not actually a pen-price claim, or if SRC_006's residual arithmetic is shown to be self-consistent under a different parsing.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"07a139da8aba6e75c1856f9a57cd772bffa92bc4248d295dcda03d20d42143f9"},{"role":"dependence","target":"abd359e177a4f9fa6816853b7af1a93547f54b656adddc2e2e05bb3c424f4992"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0d3303c073506b48e3330c9da315a94f77fd9cf9bcf4bf5a37fbfdb5f28461a3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"770eaa394b0ecefa294cf24590ec10b969c7a7cac1eac6b9b1dbb2a7d13e2a7a"}],"typicality":0.2}],"simulation_proposals":[]}